/// mlrepro — a generic recursive TRANSFORMER that THREADS A CLOSURE through its recursion declines at ≥2
/// element types (the next iterator unblock after Part C). (iterators stress test, filed 2026-07-16)
///
/// STATUS: root-caused by v-inference (queued as generic-transformer-closure-tie) — lower.rs type_specialize
/// solves a bare-closure argument IN ISOLATION (solved_lambda_arrow from the closure's own body) rather than
/// against the callee's INSTANTIATED parameter type at this call; an identity closure `fn (s) => s` leaves
/// `s` = Any → declines. Fix planned: unify `(-> Any R)` against the concrete param at the call before the
/// Any-decline. Kept here so it becomes a passing regression the tick the fix lands. (Filed durably to the
/// shared tree — it previously lived only in a v-iterators worktree; v-inference could not find it.)
///
/// RELATION TO PART C. Part C tied a recursive-generic PRODUCER's result element to its argument,
/// unblocking from-list + closure-FREE consumers/transformers (giter.cdz: count/collect/nth/last/chain/
/// take/drop/zip all compose per element type today). THIS is the closure-carrying analogue. Precisely
/// isolated:
///   - count/collect (recursive-generic, NO closure) at 2 types → FINE (Part C).
///   - a recursive-generic transformer that rebuilds GIter(a) with NO closure (e.g. an identity `idt`) at 2
///     types → FINE. So it is NOT "any recursive-generic transformer".
///   - map(it, f) / filter(it, p) (THREAD the closure f/p through the recursive call) at 2 types → CDZ0201.
///   - map at ONE element type → compiles AND runs. Each instantiation is fine alone; the SECOND fails to
///     monomorphize — the Part C symptom, for a closure-threading transformer.
///   - CHECK vs LOWER split (same as Part C): `cdz check` PASSES (exit 0); the decline is at LOWERING.
///
/// EXPECTED: map/filter (a recursive-generic transformer threading a closure) monomorphizes independently
/// per element type, like count/collect already do — opening the whole generic transformer family
/// (map / filter / take-while / scan / zip-with).

type GIter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, GIter(a))

def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)

/// The transformer that threads a closure `f` through its recursion — the shape that declines at ≥2 types.
def map(it, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(f(h), map(rest, f))

/// FACE 1: `map` over Int64 (a -> a). Compiles + runs ALONE.
@test
def map-over-int() =
  if count(map(from-list([1, 2, 3]), fn(x) => x + 1)) == 3 then unit else trap("map int")

/// FACE 2: `map` over String, SAME PROGRAM. The second instantiation triggers CDZ0201 — the closure-
/// threading transformer fails to monomorphize at a second element type.
@test
def map-over-string() =
  if count(map(from-list(["a", "b"]), fn(s) => s)) == 2 then unit else trap("map str")
